Subject: [PATCH] WowBox64: a suspended thread holds no lock and shows its live x86 context

.NET's garbage collector suspends threads at any point of their code, then reads and sets their
context. Two things went wrong under WowBox64, and the official PD2 launcher (a .NET program)
stopped in about one start of five:
- A thread suspended while it held one of Box64's locks (or the process heap's, in Box64's own
  allocations) kept it; the suspending thread then needed that lock to run its own code (to
  translate a block, change a page's protection, allocate) and both waited for ever. Wine suspends
  a thread with SIGUSR1: it stays blocked while a thread holds such a lock (mypthread.h, the heap
  wrappers in os_wine.c), so the thread stops once it lets go of the last one.
- Wine reports the x86 context in the thread's CPU area, which WowBox64 writes only at a system
  call; for a thread in translated code the collector read a stale EIP and ESP. BTCpuGetContext now
  rebuilds the live registers of a thread stopped in translated code from its ARM64 context (as
  BTCpuResetToConsistentState does for an exception, with adjust_arch's flags, SSE and x87 values,
  ported to Wine's CONTEXT); a thread stopped in Box64's own code runs on a moment and is stopped
  again. BTCpuSetContext makes such a thread start again from the context set: its native context
  goes back to the one BTCpuSimulate captured before it entered the translated code.
